<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Coverage settings for `dotnet test /collect:"XPlat Code Coverage"` (coverlet.collector).

  Passed explicitly with the `settings` switch, by CI and by Tools/Tasks.cs alike, so the number
  the gate enforces is the same a developer sees locally. Without this file coverlet also
  instruments the test assemblies themselves and the generated EF migrations, which moves the
  percentage for reasons that have nothing to do with the code under test.

  Note for editors: an XML comment may not contain a double hyphen, so command-line switches are
  written here without their leading dashes. `dotnet test` rejects the whole file if that rule is
  broken — and, as of SDK 10.0.300, still exits 0 while running no tests at all.
-->
<RunSettings>

  <DataCollectionRunSettings>
    <DataCollectors>
      <DataCollector friendlyName="XPlat code coverage">
        <Configuration>

          <!-- Cobertura only. The gate and the job summary both parse this shape. -->
          <Format>cobertura</Format>

          <!-- Test assemblies are not the subject of the measurement.

               A wildcard rather than one entry per project, because the enumerated form went
               stale: a test project added later was simply not in the list, and nothing said so.
               Every test assembly in this repository ends in "Tests", so this pattern cannot be
               outgrown by adding one.

               Stated precisely, because the previous wording overclaimed: coverlet already
               declines to instrument the assembly under test, so this filter is not what keeps
               test code out of the total today. It is the guard for the case coverlet does not
               cover on its own — a test assembly pulled in as a reference by another test
               project, which would otherwise be measured as if it were product code. -->
          <Exclude>[*Tests]*</Exclude>

          <!-- Code a tool wrote, excluded by where it lives rather than by the attribute it
               carries.

               Migrations: EF Core writes them, they are asserted on by being applied rather than
               by line coverage, and their size would otherwise dominate the total.

               Anything under obj/: every source generator writes there and nowhere else, so the
               path is an unambiguous statement that nobody typed the file. In this repository that
               is one 7,580-line file from the OpenAPI XML-comment generator in AppTemplate.Api,
               and the logging partial the LoggerMessage generator writes for
               AppTemplate.Infrastructure.Identity's SecurityEventLog.

               This replaces GeneratedCodeAttribute in the attribute list below, and the swap was
               measured rather than assumed: every assembly's covered and total line counts are
               identical either way, except the one the attribute was silently losing. -->
          <ExcludeByFile>**/Migrations/*.cs,**/obj/**/*.cs</ExcludeByFile>

          <!-- Compiler-synthesised members: record Equals/GetHashCode/ToString/Deconstruct,
               iterator and async state machines. Not code anybody wrote or can test directly.

               GeneratedCodeAttribute is deliberately NOT in this list. With coverlet 10.0.1, an
               assembly holding a source-generated partial class is dropped from the report
               entirely — not the generated half, the whole module — so
               AppTemplate.Infrastructure.Identity was absent from the coverage of its own 187 unit
               tests, and the gate scored it 0 while every one of them passed. The ExcludeByFile
               glob above covers the same code without losing the module. Adding the attribute back
               reintroduces the loss; verify against a run of
               Tests/Infrastructure/AppTemplate.Infrastructure.Identity.UnitTests alone, whose
               report must contain an AppTemplate.Infrastructure.Identity package. -->
          <ExcludeByAttribute>CompilerGeneratedAttribute,ExcludeFromCodeCoverageAttribute</ExcludeByAttribute>

          <!-- Trivial `{ get; set; }` accessors. Counting them rewards DTOs and dilutes the
               signal from the branches that hold real decisions. -->
          <SkipAutoProps>true</SkipAutoProps>

        </Configuration>
      </DataCollector>
    </DataCollectors>
  </DataCollectionRunSettings>

</RunSettings>
